⚠️ **IMPORTANT: engine disposal only happens on the success path**

The teardown comment says it plainly: "a live engine past the test end wedges the runner". But `engine.dispose()` runs only if every `expect` above it passes. Any mid-test failure (a flaky timing assert, a throw from `_countEnclosedSkyPixels`, a boot timeout) leaves the JsAppEngine running with its rAF/interval callbacks on the real event loop — which can hang or wedge the whole `flutter test` invocation and mask the real failure with a timeout.

Register the dispose as a tearDown right after `engine.start()` so it runs on every exit path:

```dart
await engine.start();
var disposed = false;
addTearDown(() async {
  if (disposed) return;
  disposed = true;
  await engine.dispose();
});
```

and keep the explicit `await engine.dispose();` in the body guarded by the same flag (or drop it, since the tearDown covers it). The trailing `attachRootWidget(SizedBox.shrink())` frame-drain can stay on the success path only — it is cosmetic compared to cancelling the JS engine's timers.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `builtWidgets` grows unboundedly and is only used as a non-emptiness flag**

Every tree rebuild appends the built widget tree (~900 frames across boot + phases), retaining each tree for the whole test for no diagnostic benefit beyond the single `isNotEmpty` assert (the failure-reason interpolation already uses `frameworkErrors`). Swap the list for a boolean:

```dart
var builtWidgetOnce = false;
// in builder:
builtWidgetOnce = true;
// at the assert:
expect(builtWidgetOnce, isTrue, ...);
```

Saves memory headroom in an already long smoke run; `frameworkErrors` remains the real diagnostic payload.
